config TT_BH_ARC_PCIE_LTSSM_LOG
	bool "PCIe LTSSM training log"
	help
	  Record PCIe LTSSM state transitions into a CSM ring buffer that the
	  host can read back, for debugging link training failures, speed
	  downgrades and recovery storms. The buffer address is published to
	  scratch register 24 and its size to 25.

	  Blackhole is a PCIe endpoint, so on switch-attached boards the link
	  is trained by host BIOS long after PCIe init runs, and the events
	  worth seeing - link up, speed changes, recovery and EQ - keep
	  happening afterwards. Recording therefore runs on its own thread,
	  indefinitely, and pcie_init() is not delayed at all: the chip boots
	  normally and brings the link up, which is the point.

	  The thread runs at the lowest application priority and busy-polls,
	  so it consumes cycles the idle thread would otherwise take and can
	  miss transitions while descheduled. Intended for debug builds.

if TT_BH_ARC_PCIE_LTSSM_LOG

config TT_BH_ARC_PCIE_LTSSM_LOG_ENTRIES
	int "LTSSM log capacity in entries"
	default 4096
	range 8 32768
	help
	  Number of transitions the ring holds, shared across both PCIe
	  instances. Each entry occupies 16 bytes of CSM, so the default costs
	  64 KiB. CSM is largely unused, and a build with roughly 160 KiB
	  spare can take 8192 entries; the linker will fail loudly if the
	  region cannot fit the choice.

	  The ring retains the most recent transitions, and consecutive
	  repeats of a state - or of a two-state alternation such as Detect
	  churn - are collapsed into a repeat count rather than consuming
	  entries, so this covers far more elapsed time than its entry count
	  suggests.

endif # TT_BH_ARC_PCIE_LTSSM_LOG
